ering law and pushed a feed-in tariff. These are clean-energy measures, and the annex counts them as `adjacent` for this topic.\n- **November 2025 newsletter and the February 2026 press release and newsletter:** in his own words, he opposes letting utilities bill customers for natural gas plants while they are being built, and the coal-ash cleanup charges. These are about utility rates and power-plant fuel, not production, so they are `adjacent`. The farmland bill he criticises (it could allow oil refineries) is about siting and local control; refineries are use, not production.\n- **Indiana Daily Student excerpt:** only a reporter's summary of the forum, with no words of his on fossil fuels, so it is `not-evidence`.\n\nI recorded every statement as `statement-other` and `in-term`, and left `rests_on` and `quotes` empty. Under `needs_source` I asked for his own words or record on Indiana oil and gas permits, coal mining or state-land leasing.\n\nOne judgement call on dates: the February press release page shows only \"Feb 25\" with no year. I recorded 2026-02-25 because his newsletter of 2026-02-28 reprints the same text.","ttft_ms":15246,"type":"result","duration_ms":37795,"uuid":"f29aa9c3-a034-4cce-9c36-641282b43125","ttft_stream_ms":5978,"time_to_request_ms":274,"first_content_frame_ms":5978,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
